  "theory": {
    "intro": "The power rule fails at $n=-1$, so $\\dfrac{1}{x}$ has its own primitive: $\\int\\dfrac{1}{x}\\,dx = \\ln|x| + C$. For a linear denominator, $\\int\\dfrac{1}{ax+b}\\,dx = \\dfrac{1}{a}\\ln|ax+b| + C$, where $a\\ne0$ and $ax+b\\ne0$. The modulus lets the rule work for negative $x$ too.",
    "facts": [
"$\\int\\dfrac{1}{x}\\,dx = \\ln|x| + C$ for $x \\ne 0$, because $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\ln|x| = \\dfrac{1}{x}$.",
"The **modulus** is needed because $\\ln x$ exists only for $x>0$, while $\\dfrac{1}{x}$ has area under it for negative $x$ too.",
"A constant factor comes straight out: $\\int\\dfrac{k}{x}\\,dx = k\\ln|x| + C$.",
"For a linear denominator, divide by the coefficient of $x$: $\\int\\dfrac{1}{ax+b}\\,dx = \\dfrac{1}{a}\\ln|ax+b| + C$, where $a\\ne0$ and $ax+b\\ne0$.",
"Forgetting the $\\dfrac{1}{a}$ is the usual slip, so differentiate your answer to check it.",
"A negative coefficient of $x$ gives a negative factor, as in $\\int\\dfrac{1}{3-x}\\,dx = -\\ln|3-x| + C$.",
"$\\int\\dfrac{1}{5x}\\,dx$ may be written $\\dfrac{1}{5}\\ln|x| + C$ or $\\dfrac{1}{5}\\ln|5x| + C$, since the two differ by a constant.",
"A definite integral is undefined when $x=0$ for $\\dfrac{1}{x}$, or $x=-\\dfrac{b}{a}$ for $\\dfrac{1}{ax+b}$, lies inside the interval or at either endpoint.",
"Split a fraction such as $\\dfrac{x+3}{x}$ into $1 + \\dfrac{3}{x}$ first, because there is no quotient rule for integration."
],
    "steps": [
"Split fractions where needed, then identify the rule for each resulting term.",
"Take constant factors outside the integral sign.",
"Integrate each term separately: for a reciprocal term, write $\\ln$ of the modulus of the denominator, divided by the coefficient of $x$; for other powers, use the power rule with $n\\ne-1$.",
"Add $+C$, or first check that no denominator is zero inside the interval or at either endpoint, then substitute the limits as $\\left[F(x)\\right]_{a}^{b}$ for a definite integral."
]
  },
